# Scala Windsurf Rules

This rules file defines standards for modern Scala development, adhering to the latest Scala language features, industry best practices, clean architecture principles, and self-documenting code conventions.

## Syntax & Language Features

### Use Modern Scala
- Prefer Scala 3 features (given/using, enums, opaque types, extension methods)
- Use immutable collections and values (`val`) by default; use `var` only when mutation is necessary
- Use case classes for immutable data structures
- Prefer pattern matching and destructuring for clarity
- Use for-comprehensions for sequencing computations (especially with `Option`, `Either`, `Future`)
- Leverage type inference and avoid redundant type annotations
- Prefer top-level definitions and package objects over deep nesting

### Variable Declarations
- Use `val` for immutable values, `var` only when mutation is required
- Prefer pattern matching for multiple assignments
- Use default parameters and named arguments for clarity
- Avoid null; use `Option` for optional values

### Functions
- Prefer pure functions and avoid side effects
- Use anonymous functions (lambdas) for short-lived logic
- Keep functions small and focused on a single responsibility
- Use early returns to reduce nesting
- Use parameter lists for dependencies (favor dependency injection)
- Prefer extension methods for enriching types

### Classes & Objects
- Use case classes for data modeling
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Use sealed traits for closed hierarchies
- Leverage private and protected modifiers for encapsulation
- Use companion objects for factory methods and utilities
- Use implicit classes/type classes for ad-hoc polymorphism

### Asynchronous & Effectful Code
- Use `Future` or effect types (e.g., cats.effect.IO) for async operations
- Prefer for-comprehensions for composing effects
- Handle errors explicitly with `Try`, `Either`, or effect types
- Avoid blocking operations in async code

## Clean Code Principles

### Naming
- Use descriptive, intention-revealing names
- camelCase for variables and methods, PascalCase for classes/traits/objects
- UPPERCASE for constants
- Avoid abbreviations unless universally known

### Function Design
- Functions should do one thing and do it well
- Limit parameters (prefer case classes for grouping)
- Avoid side effects; return new values instead of mutating state
- Use recursion or higher-order functions over loops

### Comments & Documentation
- Write self-documenting code; minimize comments
- Use ScalaDoc for public APIs and complex logic
- Document the 'why', not the 'what'
- Keep documentation updated

## Clean Architecture

### Module Structure
- Organize code by feature/domain, not by type
- Separate business logic from side effects (e.g., IO, DB)
- Use dependency inversion for flexibility
- Favor modular, testable components

### Application Structure
- Maintain clear boundaries between layers
- Use unidirectional data flow
- Apply single responsibility principle
- Separate configuration from implementation

### State Management
- Prefer immutable state
- Use ADTs (sealed traits + case classes) for state modeling
- Isolate side effects
- Use Option/Either for error and missing value handling

## Performance & Optimization

### Collection Performance
- Choose the right collection type for the task (List, Vector, Map, Set)
- Use lazy collections where appropriate
- Avoid unnecessary allocations

### Concurrency
- Use Futures or effect types for concurrency
- Avoid shared mutable state
- Use proper synchronization primitives if necessary

## Error Handling

### Robust Error Management
- Use sealed trait hierarchies for error types
- Prefer `Either`, `Try`, or effect types for error handling
- Provide descriptive error messages
- Never swallow exceptions silently

### Defensive Programming
- Validate inputs
- Use guards and pattern matching
- Prefer total functions; handle all cases

## Testing Standards

### Test Coverage
- Write unit tests for all business logic using ScalaTest or specs2
- Use property-based testing for invariants
- Test error conditions and edge cases

### Test Structure
- Use AAA pattern (Arrange, Act, Assert)
- Keep tests isolated and independent
- Use descriptive test names

## Security Considerations

### Secure Coding Practices
- Sanitize inputs
- Avoid unsafe reflection and dynamic code execution
- Use proper authentication and authorization
- Avoid storing sensitive data in plain text

## Documentation

### Code Documentation
- Document public APIs with ScalaDoc
- Include usage examples
- Maintain a changelog and architecture notes

### Repository Documentation
- Maintain a comprehensive README
- Document setup, development, and architecture decisions
- Keep documentation current with code changes

## Idiomatic Scala Rules

- Prefer algebraic data types (sealed traits + case classes) for modeling closed sets of possibilities.
- Use `Option`, `Either`, or custom ADTs instead of exceptions for expected error handling.
- Avoid using null; embrace `Option` and safe alternatives.
- Use pattern matching exhaustively—always cover all cases, even with a wildcard or `sealed` trait.
- Prefer for-comprehensions for sequencing computations involving `Option`, `Either`, `Future`, or custom monads.
- Use type classes and implicits for ad-hoc polymorphism; prefer context bounds for simple cases.
- Avoid mutable state; if mutation is necessary, confine it to the smallest possible scope.
- Use companion objects for constructors, factory methods, and type class instances.
- Use extension methods (Scala 3) or implicit classes (Scala 2) to enrich types.
- Prefer expressive, type-safe APIs—leverage the type system to prevent invalid states.
- Use higher-order functions (`map`, `flatMap`, `fold`, etc.) over explicit recursion or loops.
- Avoid side effects in constructors; use pure data structures and effectful computations for side effects.
- Use `import` statements locally (inside methods or classes) to reduce scope and avoid namespace pollution.
- Prefer `final` for classes that are not intended to be extended.
- Use `private[this]` for the most restrictive visibility when possible.
- Avoid “magic numbers” and hardcoded strings—use constants or enumerations.
- Use `lazy val` for expensive computations that should only be evaluated once, when needed.
- Prefer `Vector` or `List` for immutable sequences; use `Array` only for performance-critical code.
- Use `Map` and `Set` from the immutable collections by default.
- Leverage `@tailrec` annotation for recursive methods to ensure stack safety.
- Use `Try` for exception-handling in effectful computations, but prefer `Either` or `Validated` for domain errors.
- Prefer `def` for methods, `val` for constants, and `lazy val` for lazy initialization.

## Pure Functional Scala Rules

- Favor pure functions: every function should return the same output for the same input and have no side effects.
- Avoid mutable state entirely—use only immutable data structures and values.
- Model data with algebraic data types (sealed traits + case classes), not classes with mutable fields or inheritance.
- Eliminate nulls; represent absence with `Option` and errors with `Either` or custom ADTs.
- Use higher-order functions (`map`, `flatMap`, `fold`, `traverse`, etc.) for all data transformations.
- Prefer function composition (`andThen`, `compose`, or chaining) over method calls on objects.
- Use type classes and context bounds for polymorphism, not inheritance.
- Pass dependencies explicitly as parameters (including type class instances), not via fields or constructors.
- Use effect types (e.g., `cats.effect.IO`, `ZIO`, or `Future`) to model side effects; never perform effects directly.
- Express all effects (I/O, randomness, time, state, etc.) in the type signature.
- Prefer recursion and tail recursion over loops; use the `@tailrec` annotation for safety.
- Use pattern matching and exhaustive case analysis for all ADTs.
- Avoid constructors with logic or side effects; use pure factory functions.
- Structure code as modules of functions and values, not as classes or objects with mutable state.
- Use `val` and `def` for values and functions; avoid `var` and mutable collections.
- Avoid the `new` keyword except for case classes or when required by libraries.
- Use local imports and explicit dependencies to keep functions pure and testable.
- Prefer referential transparency: replaceable expressions with their values without changing program behavior.
- Use libraries like Cats, Cats Effect, or ZIO for functional abstractions and effect management.
- Document laws and invariants for type classes and abstractions.

## Testing Rules

- All code must be accompanied by tests; never commit untested code.
- Use property-based testing (e.g., ScalaCheck) to verify invariants and general properties.
- Prefer pure functions for easy and reliable testing.
- Use ScalaTest, MUnit, or specs2 for unit and integration tests.
- Structure tests to follow the AAA pattern (Arrange, Act, Assert).
- Keep tests isolated, independent, and deterministic; avoid shared mutable state.
- Use descriptive, intention-revealing test names.
- Test edge cases, boundary conditions, and error scenarios, not just the happy path.
- Mock or stub external dependencies and side effects using appropriate libraries (e.g., Mockito, cats-effect-testing).
- Write tests for type class laws and invariants where applicable.
- Use fixtures and generators for reusable test data.
- Prefer in-memory or embedded resources for integration tests.
- Maintain high test coverage, but prioritize meaningful tests over coverage metrics.
- Keep test code clean, readable, and as idiomatic as production code.
- Test effectful code using effect-testing libraries (e.g., cats-effect-testing, zio-test).
- Run tests automatically in CI/CD pipelines and before each commit.
- Document known test limitations and expected failures.

## Documentation Rules

- Maintain a comprehensive README with setup instructions, development guidelines, and architecture decisions.
- Document setup, development, and architecture decisions in README.
- Keep documentation current with code changes.
- Document known limitations and expected failures in README.
